\section{Background}\label{sec:background}

\subsection{Synthesizer inversion \& sound matching}

Given an audio signal, the sound matching task aims to find a synthesizer parameter configuration that best approximates it~\cite{roth_comparison_2011,shier_synthesizer_2021}.
% The task has received continued research attention over multiple decades and remains an active area of research.
% This difficulty is largely due to the nature of audio synthesizers, which are typically highly non-linear with respect to their parameters, between which there exist non-trivial multiscale interactions.
% Further, as we explore in this work, the task is often intrinsically ill-posed.
%
We focus in this paper on \textit{synthesizer inversion}, a sub-task of sound matching in which the audio signal we seek to approximate is known \textit{a priori} to have come from the synthesizer.
We do so to eliminate confounding factors due to the non-trivial implicit projection from general audio signals to the set of signals producible by the synthesizer.
% General sound matching implies a projection from the set of all audio signals to the set of those producible by the synthesizer.
% This map is non-trivial in itself, and depends on numerous design choices, including the selection of an appropriate audio metric.
% Excluding this step thus eliminates a source of confounding factors and allows us to isolate the effect of symmetry.
% Nonetheless, prior work often optimizes for the inversion task and addresses sound matching by simply applying the resulting model to out-of-distribution inputs~\cite{bruford_synthesizer_2024,barkan_inversynth_2019}, and so we also report results on such data, though this is not our focus.
% works effectively optimize for inversion, and hope to address sound matching by generalization. methods exist for training using signal processor gradients or their approximations, but these introduce other challenges.

For an overview of historical sound matching approaches, we refer the reader to Shier's~\cite{shier_synthesizer_2021} comprehensive review.
% Importantly, they delineate between \textit{search} and \textit{model-based} methods, where the latter includes neural network based methods.
% Here, we further divide neural network approaches into two groups: \textit{regression-based}, which deterministically produce a point estimate, and \textit{generative}, which produce a sample from the conditional distribution of parameters given an audio signal.
The state-of-the-art in regression-based approaches was recently proposed by Bruford et al~\cite{bruford_synthesizer_2024}, who proposed to adopt the audio spectrogram transformer~\cite{gong_ast_2021} architecture.
Given its superior performance over MLP and CNN models, we adopt this model as our regression baseline.

Esling et al~\cite{esling_flow_2020} presented the first generative approach, which was subsequently extended by Le Vaillant et al~\cite{vaillant_improving_2021}.
These methods train variational autoencoders on audio spectrograms, enriching the posterior distribution with normalizing flows.
A second flow is jointly trained with a regression loss to predict synthesizer parameters from this learned audio representation.
