\subsection{Continuous normalizing flows}

Continuous normalizing flows (CNFs)~\cite{chen_neural_2018,grathwohl_ffjord_2018} are a family of powerful generative models which define invertible, continuous transformations between probability distributions.
% They are effectively the continuous-time analogue of discrete normalizing flows~\cite{rezende_variational_2015} and share a deep similarity with diffusion models~\cite{gong_interpreting_2021,huang_variational_2021}.
% Formally, a CNF over $\bbR^d$ is a map $\phi: [0, 1] \times \bbR^d \to \bbR^d$ which acts on probability densities via the pushforward operator $\ast$:
%
% \begin{equation}
%   [\phi_t]_\ast p(x) = p(\phi^{-1}_t(x))\det\left[\frac{\partial \phi_t^{-1}}{\partial x} (x)\right],
% \end{equation}
%
% % \noindent adopting the notation $\square_t(x) \triangleq \square(t, x)$ for maps with domain $[0, 1] \times \bbR^d$.
% \noindent The flow $\phi$ can be linked to a time-varying vector field $u: [0, 1] \times \bbR^d \to \bbR^d$ by the ODE $d\phi = u dt$. 
% CNF models are trained by approximating $u_t$ with a neural network $v_\theta(t, \cdot)$.
%
% Let $p: [0, 1] \times \bbR^d \to \bbR^+$ define a space of time dependent probability measures on $\bbR^d$ such that for some initial distribution $p_0$ we have $p_t = [\phi(t, \cdot)]_\ast p_0$.
% If $p$ satisfies the continuity equation, i.e. $\frac{\partial p}{\partial t} = -\text{div}(p_t u_t)$, then we can say that $p_t$ is a \textit{probability path} and $u$ is its \textit{probability flow} ODE.
% Let $p_0$ be a distribution from which we can draw samples, and let $p_1$ denote our data distribution.
% Our task is to find a vector field $u_t$ that defines a flow from $p_0$ to $p_1$.
% This is typically achieved by approximating $u_t$ with a neural network $v_\theta(t, \cdot)$.
%
% Maximum likelihood training of CNFs \todo{CITE} relies on expensive numerical integration of the vector field network and so scales poorly in practice.
The conditional flow matching~\cite{tong_improving_2024} framework allows us to train CNFs without expensive numerical integration by sampling a \textit{conditional} probability path and regressing a closed form vector field which, in expectation, recovers the exact same gradients as regressing over the marginal field~\cite{lipman_flow_2023,tong_improving_2024}.
In this work, we adopt the rectified flow~\cite{liu_flow_2023} probability path which we pair with a minibatch approximation to the optimal transport coupling~\cite{pooladian_multisample_2023}.
We build on prior work on equivariant flows~\cite{song_equivariant_2023,hassan_equivariant_2024,klein_equivariant_2023} which are known to produce samples from invariant distributions~\cite{kohler_equivariant_2020}.
% In the short time since the introduction of the flow matching framework, numerous variations have been proposed, including alternate conditional probability paths~\cite{tong_improving_2024,liu_flow_2023}, optimal transport minibatch couplings~\cite{tong_improving_2024,pooladian_multisample_2023}, formulations over hybrid discrete-continouous spaces~\cite{dunn_mixed_2024}, and, of particular relevance to our work, equivariant flows~\cite{song_equivariant_2023,hassan_equivariant_2024,klein_equivariant_2023} which exactly parameterize invariant distributions~\cite{kohler_equivariant_2020}.

