The implications of our findings are clear: if the synthesizer has a symmetry, it is better to (i) approach the problem generatively and (ii) learn the corresponding invariant density.
This extends beyond synthesizers, as audio effects also commonly exhibit permutation symmetries, as noted by Nercessian~\cite{nercessian_neural_2020}.
Beyond audio, these results are of relevance anywhere neural networks parameterize an external system with structural symmetries.
