\title{A Matlab-based Toolbox for Automatic EMT Modeling and Small-Signal Stability Analysis of 
%Hybrid AC/DC
Modern Power Systems}
%\thanks{Thanks \dots} 
\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Josep Ar\'{e}valo-Soler,~\IEEEmembership{Student Member,~IEEE}, Dionysios Moutevelis,~\IEEEmembership{Member,~IEEE}, Elia Mateu-Barriendos, \\ Onur Alican,~\IEEEmembership{Student Member,~IEEE}, Carlos Collados-Rodr\'{i}guez,  Marc Cheah-Mañe,~\IEEEmembership{Senior Member,~IEEE}, \\ Eduardo Prieto-Araujo,~\IEEEmembership{Senior Member,~IEEE}, and Oriol Gomis-Bellmunt,~\IEEEmembership{Fellow,~IEEE}}}
%~\IEEEmembership{Member,~IEEE}
%\IEEEauthorblockA{}
\vspace{-0.8cm}
%
\maketitle
%
\begin{abstract}
%
The intensive integration of power converters is changing the way that power systems operate, leading to the emergence of new types of dynamic phenomena and instabilities.
%
%These phenomena have augmented the frequency spectrum of interest for stability analysis from a few hertz up to the kilohertz range.
%
At the same time, converters act as an interface between traditional AC grids and their more recent DC counterparts, giving rise to hybrid AC/DC networks.
%
These conditions increase the necessity for stability analysis tools that can simultaneously account for the newly-introduced dynamic phenomena
%of high frequencies
and can also be applied for the stability study of hybrid networks.
%
%, increasing the necessity for appropriate analysis tools.
%that consider all these new aspects.
%
This paper presents a Matlab-based toolbox for small-signal analysis of hybrid AC/DC power systems considering electromagnetic-transient (EMT) models.
%
The toolbox allows the automatized modeling of the system from the input data and offers options for modal, impedance and passivity analyses.
%
%Contrary to other existing software solutions, the presented toolbox considers the high frequency dynamics of the power network, allowing accurate stability assessment of converter-based grids.
%
In the paper, the structure and internal processes of the toolbox are duly discussed, together with all its features, both main and complementary.
%
Its capabilities for stability analysis are demonstrated via comprehensive case studies of converter-based system of various size and topology.
%
%Therefore, correct modeling of the power system has become increasingly important.
%
%This has promoted the development of new tools for analyzing modern power systems, as presented in this paper.
%
%Some of the newly observed interaction phenomena can be studied with small-signal analysis, which forms the core of the proposed tool.
%
%In this new paradigm, it is essential to properly model the grid elements accurately, considering the electromagnetic frequency range.
%
%This paper presents a MATLAB-based toolbox for conducting small-signal analysis of modern power systems considering electromagnetic-transient (EMT) models.

\end{abstract}
%
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Small-signal Analysis, Electromagnetic Transient Model, Hybrid AC/DC System, Impedance Analysis, Passivity Analysis
\end{IEEEkeywords}
%
\vspace{-0.2cm}
%
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec.introduction}
%
\subsection{Motivation}
%
Modern power systems are experiencing a deep transformation as a result of the massive integration of~\acp{ibr}.
%renewables, the proliferation of electrical mobility, and the increasing electrification of industry.
%The former traditional centralized generation approach with uniform power flows is evolving to more distributed generation installed in transmission and distribution systems.
%
Power electronics are being used massively in industrial and domestic loads, renewable generation units (mainly solar photovoltaics, onshore and offshore wind), Battery Energy Storage Systems (BESS), Flexible AC transmission systems (FACTS), and \ac{hvdc} transmission systems.
%
Power electronics-based systems have different characteristics compared to \acp{sg}, introducing dynamic phenomena on a much faster timescale~\cite{milano2018foundations}.
%
At the same time, they can act as an interface with DC networks, enabling the operation of hybrid AC/DC systems on both the transmission and the distribution level~\cite{van2016hvdc,harrison2024review}.
%
For these reasons, modern software for power system stability analysis needs to adapt to the evolving landscape introduced by~\acp{ibr}.
%
%Converters are programmable units that obey the implemented code, and their behavior is defined by the control laws. Additionally, converters cannot be overloaded significantly, and the controller limits the current to ensure safe operation. Converters lack the physical inertia of synchronous machines, but they can emulate it by implementing the so-called synthetic inertia. %Converters can be programmed in grid forming or grid following roles [xx]. 
%
% The stability analysis of modern power systems needs to consider all new predefined units, together with the classic ones. New types of instabilities associated with converters are being identified [xxx] suggesting an update of the classic classifications of stability [xx]. Some of the instabilities involve the interaction between some passive components of the electrical lines and inner states of the converter controls [xx]. This suggests the importance of appropriate modeling on networks and control when doing small-signal stability analysis. Most commercial software solutions [xxx] assume that the lines and some internal electric components can be modeled with algebraic equations when conducting small signal stability analysis. In the present work, we model all electrical circuits with differential equations to ensure that we capture all relevant dynamic information. The developed tool can use both approaches, and in some cases, it is shown that the RMS approach (algebraic equations for electrical circuits) fails to identify important instabilities captured by the EMT approach (differential equations for electrical circuits). 
%
%Contrary to the defined modern power systems, the stability and dynamics of traditional power systems were dominated by the operation of and interaction between synchronous generators.
%
\subsection{Discussion on RMS vs EMT Modal Analysis}
%
In traditional power systems, the slow dynamics of \acp{sg} were considered the  most relevant, and potentially most hazardous for the proper power system operation~\cite{kundur2004definition}.
%
These dynamics were the driving force behind the most common undesirable phenomena, such as local and inter-area oscillations between generators and loss of synchronism during transients~\cite{kundur}.
%typically manifesting in the sub-synchronous timescale, i.e., a timescale way below the network nominal frequency~\cite{kundur}.
%(typically 50 or 60 Hz).
%
The electromagnetic dynamics of the generators and the network, typically concerning frequencies much higher than the nominal one, were considered sufficiently damped and thus, not threatening to the stability of the system.
%
For this reason, these dynamics were simplified as algebraic equations in the models used for \ac{tds} and stability analysis, resulting in the classical \ac{dae} formulation for the power system.
%
This modeling approach is known as \ac{rms}, phasor-domain, or quasi-static phasor approach~\cite{lacerda2023phasor,lara2023revisiting}.
%

