\subsection{Farthest Point Sampling}
% Farthest point sampling is a technique used in various computational geometry and graphics applications. It involves iteratively selecting points from a dataset such that each new point is as far as possible from the previously selected points. This method ensures that the selected points are well-spaced and can be used to approximate shapes, surfaces, or distributions effectively. Farthest point sampling is particularly useful in scenarios where a representative subset of data points is needed, such as in mesh generation, surface simplification, or cluster initialization.
% The main drawback of farthest point sampling (FPS) is its relatively time-consuming process. The computational complexity of FPS is \( O(NC) \), where \( N \) represents the number of points in the point cloud, and \( C \) is the number of points to be sampled. This complexity can become significant, especially with large datasets or when a high number of samples is required.

Farthest point sampling (FPS) is a technique widely employed in computational geometry and computer graphics applications. It is designed to iteratively select points from a dataset such that each newly selected point is maximally distant from the previously chosen points. This method can be formally expressed as follows:

\begin{equation}
p_{i+1} = \arg\max_{p \in P} \left( \min_{p_j \in S} d(p, p_j) \right), 
\end{equation}

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \( P \) represents the set of points in the point cloud,
    \item \( S \) denotes the subset of points that have already been selected,
    \item \( d(p, p_j) \) is the distance between points \( p \) and \( p_j \),
    \item \( p_{i+1} \) is the next point selected such that its minimum distance to any point in \( S \) is maximized.
\end{itemize}

This sampling strategy ensures that the selected points are well-distributed, making it effective for approximating shapes, surfaces, or distributions. FPS is particularly useful in applications requiring a representative subset of data points, such as mesh generation, surface simplification, or cluster initialization.


The time complexity of a typical FPS method~\cite{10.5555/3295222.3295263,9919246} is \( O(kn) \), where \( n \) is the total number of points in the dataset, and \( k \) denotes the number of points to be sampled. 
The latest FPS method QuickFPS~\cite{10122654} organizes large-scale point clouds into multiple bins. By employing two mechanisms—merge computation and implicit computation on these bins—it significantly reduces external memory access and computation costs, thereby improving the speed of farthest point sampling.
