\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cut}
    Given a graph $G=(V,E)$ and an MEG set $M$ of $G$, if $c\in M$ is a cut-vertex of $G$, then $M\setminus\{c\}$ is also an MEG set of $G$. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    We prove this lemma by contradiction. For the sake of contradiction, let $M\setminus\{c\}$ is not an MEG set of $G$. This implies that an edge $uv$ exists, such that $uv$ is only monitored by $c$ and $x$ (where $x$ is some other vertex of $M$). Let $C_1,C_2,\ldots,C_k$ be the components of $G\setminus \{c\}$. Let us assume $uv$ is contained in $C_i\cup \{c\}$, hence evidently $x\in C_i$. Also note that $\vert C_j\cap M\vert>0$ for every $j\in [k]$. 

    Now consider any edge $cv'$, where $v'\in C_j$ and $j\neq i$. It is easy to observe that this edge is monitored by $c$ and some $y\in C_j$. But if every shortest path between $c$ and $x$ contains the edge $uv$, then every shortest path between $x$ and $y$ also contains the edge $uv$ (refer to figure \ref{fig:cut}). 

    \begin{figure}[h!]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[scale=0.5]{cut.eps}
        \caption{the edge $uv$ is monitored by the pair $x,y$}
        \label{fig:cut}
    \end{figure}

    Hence $uv$ is monitored by $x,y\in M\setminus \{c\}$. This leads to a contradiction; hence $M\setminus\{c\}$ is an MEG set of $G$. 
\end{proof}

